---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const claims = [
  {
    t: 'Root ships inside the signed system image, and verified boot still reports green.',
    d: 'A setuid su binary is part of the release build, so a locked bootloader and an enforcing dm-verity do not stop root over adb. Nothing had to be flashed to get it.',
    href: '/docs/platform/#boot',
    tag: 'read + probed',
  },
  {
    t: 'The tablet has no volume buttons, so the bootloader also accepts a typed unlock token.',
    d: 'Unlocking normally requires pressing a physical key to confirm. This one takes a fastboot command instead — and the unlock-permission flag lives in the last four bytes of a partition.',
    href: '/docs/platform/#boot',
    tag: 'read',
  },
  {
    t: 'The vendor can disable the device remotely, and the flag survives a factory reset.',
    d: 'A blacklist answer written into a raw calibration partition triggers a non-dismissible lock and a five-minute shutdown. No wipe touches that partition.',
    href: '/docs/platform/#killswitch',
    tag: 'read + probed',
  },
  {
    t: 'Any app on the device can act as the system user.',
    d: 'A vendor service in system_server clears the caller identity and never checks who called, and the SELinux policy lets untrusted apps reach it.',
    href: '/docs/platform/#services',
    tag: 'read + probed',
  },
  {
    t: 'One exported provider will open any file path you hand it.',
    d: 'The whole implementation is "read the path out of the URI, open it". No permission, no validation, same UID as the app that owns the AI chat history.',
    href: '/posts/audit/#providers',
    tag: 'read',
  },
  {
    t: 'The platform signing key is AOSP\u2019s public test key.',
    d: 'While the build advertises release keys, several product apps — one of them sharing the system UID — are signed with the well-known public key from AOSP.',
    href: '/docs/platform/#signing-key',
    tag: 'read + probed',
  },
  {
    t: 'The compositor hosts 70 undocumented binder codes, including a frame dump.',
    d: 'Display modes, panel bias voltage, waveform install, ghosting filter, and a debug hook that writes the current grayscale framebuffer to a PNG.',
    href: '/docs/platform/#ink-bus',
    tag: 'read (disassembly) + probed',
  },
  {
    t: 'Handwriting bypasses the window manager entirely.',
    d: 'A dedicated buffer queue carries strokes to the compositor, which pushes them to the panel driver without waiting for composition or vsync. Full-screen refreshes are deferred while the pen is down.',
    href: '/docs/platform/#ink-bus',
    tag: 'read (disassembly)',
  },
  {
    t: 'The panel\u2019s waveform is a file you can replace, guarded by a key in the same binary.',
    d: 'Twenty-three LUTs ship on disk, one per panel batch. The installer checks a SHA-256 digest that arrives AES-encrypted with a constant key and IV found in the compositor.',
    href: '/docs/platform/#waveform',
    tag: 'read + probed',
  },
  {
    t: 'The pen is a Cortex-M microcontroller with its own firmware and flasher.',
    d: 'A Wacom W9013 on I\u00B2C, flashed at every boot from an Intel HEX file in the product partition, serving its own HID descriptor. The factory image is right there on disk.',
    href: '/docs/platform/#pen',
    tag: 'read + probed',
  },
];

const bonus = {
  t: 'Bonus: the note format is documented by the app\u2019s own importer.',
  d: 'A Python function builds a .note file the tablet accepts, because the importer is a specification that fails loudly — and because the vendor\u2019s page-copy pipeline enumerates every resource a page can carry.',
  href: '/posts/note-format/',
  tag: 'read + probed',
};
---

<Base title="Ten surprises" description="Ten things this e-ink tablet does that you would not guess, each with how it was checked.">
  <h1>Ten surprises</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Things this tablet does that you would not guess from the outside. Each one links to the
    reference section behind it, with its evidence label.
  </p>

  <ol class="plain" style="list-style:none;counter-reset:c;padding:0">
    {claims.map((c, i) => (
      <li style="counter-increment:c">
        <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;align-items:baseline">
          <span class="meta date" style="min-width:1.5rem">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <div>
            <a href={c.href}><span class="claim">{c.t}</span></a>
            <p style="margin:.3rem 0 .2rem">{c.d}</p>
            <div class="meta">{c.tag}</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <hr />

  <ul class="plain">
    <li>
      <a href={bonus.href}><span class="claim">{bonus.t}</span></a>
      <p style="margin:.3rem 0 .2rem">{bonus.d}</p>
      <div class="meta">{bonus.tag}</div>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note">
    No vendor binaries, key material, identifiers or captures are published. Security items are
    described by class of problem and fix direction only — see the
    <a href="/posts/audit/">audit</a>.
  </div>
</Base>
